# SuperHeng backend — environment template
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in real values. `.env` is git-ignored
# (see ../.gitignore); this template is safe to commit.
#
#   cp .env.example .env      # macOS / Linux
#   copy .env.example .env    # Windows

# --- Supabase --------------------------------------------------------------
# Project URL — Supabase dashboard > Project Settings > Data API > Project URL.
# Shape: https://<project-ref>.supabase.co   (no trailing slash)
#
# Do NOT include the `/rest/v1` path that page displays underneath the URL.
# The client appends that segment itself, so keeping it produces
# `/rest/v1/rest/v1/lottery_draws`, PostgREST answers 404 and every query
# fails with PGRST125 ("Invalid path specified in request URL"). The symptom
# is a 500 from the data endpoints while `/api/health` still reports
# "configured", because health only checks that these two variables exist.
SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co

# Supabase API key — Project Settings > API Keys.
#
# WHICH KEY YOU NEED DEPENDS ON WHAT IS READING THIS FILE:
#
#   * scraper.py WRITES draw rows into `lottery_draws`. `schema.sql` enables
#     row-level security and defines a SELECT policy only, so there is no
#     INSERT/UPDATE policy — the `anon` key will be rejected. Ingestion needs
#     the **service_role** key, which bypasses RLS.
#
#   * main.py only needs to reach the database, so `anon` is enough there.
#
# The service_role key is a full-access admin credential for the whole project.
# It is server-side only: never put it in the Expo app, never prefix it with
# EXPO_PUBLIC_, and never commit it. Keep it out of the client bundle entirely.
SUPABASE_KEY=your-supabase-anon-or-service-role-key

# --- The Stripe webhook's database credential ------------------------------
# A **second, more powerful** key, used by exactly one endpoint:
# `POST /api/webhook/stripe`.
#
# Everything else in this API reads the database with the *caller's own* token,
# so row-level security decides what is visible. Granting a subscription cannot
# be done that way: `schema_profiles.sql` deliberately denies `subscription_tier`
# to `authenticated`, which is what stops a user pricing their own subscription
# at zero. So the one function that writes an entitlement needs a key that
# bypasses RLS.
#
# This is a full-access admin credential for the whole project. It is
# server-side only: never put it in the Expo app, never prefix it with
# EXPO_PUBLIC_, and never commit it. Without it, a verified payment cannot be
# recorded and the webhook answers 503 (so Stripe retries) instead of guessing.
#
# Dashboard > Project Settings > API Keys. Either the legacy `service_role`
# key (a JWT) or one of the newer `sb_secret_...` secret keys will do, provided
# it carries service_role privileges — an anon/publishable key will NOT work,
# because it is the column grant that stands in the way.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY=your-supabase-service-role-or-secret-key

# --- Premium entitlements ---------------------------------------------------
# Reading an entitlement needs no privileged variable — but **writing** one
# does, and that is `SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY` above. Read this section for the read
# path, and the Stripe section below for the write path.
#
# `/api/predict/premium` requires a Supabase access token (`Authorization: Bearer
# <token>`) and reads the caller's `user_profiles` row with **that token**, so RLS
# filters the row and a mistake in the backend cannot return someone else's
# subscription. The consequence is that this API holds no privileged credential at
# all: it validates the token with Supabase Auth and reads the profile as the user,
# using SUPABASE_KEY above.
#
# THEREFORE, FOR THE PREMIUM GATE TO WORK:
#   * SUPABASE_KEY must be the **anon** (publishable) key. A service_role key
#     bypasses RLS, so the profile read would still succeed -- but it would succeed
#     for *any* user id, which is exactly the safety property this design buys.
#   * `backend/schema_profiles.sql` must have been run. Without it every gated
#     request answers 503 with a message saying so rather than 403: a missing table
#     is a deployment problem, not a verdict about the caller.
#
# There is deliberately no `AUTH_DISABLED` or `ALLOW_ANY_TOKEN` switch here. A
# bypass flag on an authentication boundary is the shape of every accidental
# production payout; the tests override the FastAPI dependency instead, which no
# running deployment can reach.

# --- Stripe (payments) ------------------------------------------------------
# Cards for international customers, PromptPay for Thai ones, both through
# Stripe's hosted Checkout page.
#
# NO CLIENT-SIDE STRIPE KEY IS NEEDED. The app fetches a checkout URL from this
# API and redirects the browser to Stripe. Nothing Stripe-related runs in the
# app, so there is no publishable key (`pk_...`) anywhere in this project — see
# ../.env.example for why the `NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY` named in the
# original integration brief is deliberately absent rather than merely unset.
#
# Until BOTH variables below are set, `POST /api/checkout/create-session`
# answers the honest "no gateway connected yet" response and the app does not
# offer a purchase at all. That is deliberate: a secret key without a webhook
# secret would create real, payable sessions that could never grant access —
# the customer pays and nothing happens.

# Dashboard > Developers > API keys > Secret key. Starts with `sk_test_` or
# `sk_live_`. Server-side only: it can create charges and read every payment on
# the account.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_your-secret-key

# Dashboard > Developers > Webhooks > your endpoint > Signing secret. Starts
# with `whsec_`. **Each endpoint has its own**: `stripe listen` prints a
# *different* secret from the deployed endpoint's, and pasting the wrong one
# makes every delivery fail verification with a 400 that looks exactly like an
# attack.
#
#   Endpoint URL : https://<your-api-host>/api/webhook/stripe
#   Events       : checkout.session.completed
#                  checkout.session.async_payment_succeeded
#                  checkout.session.async_payment_failed
#                  checkout.session.expired
#
# The `async_payment_*` pair is not optional decoration: a delayed payment
# method can complete while still settling, so the grant waits for the event
# that means the money actually arrived.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_your-endpoint-signing-secret

# --- Optional payment tuning -------------------------------------------------
# Which methods Checkout offers. `card,promptpay` is the default, and is what
# the paywall advertises.
#
# PROMPTPAY NEEDS A THAILAND-REGISTERED STRIPE ACCOUNT and THB amounts, and it
# must be enabled under dashboard > Settings > Payment methods. Naming it here
# is necessary but not sufficient: the parameter this build sends filters
# Stripe's *dynamically eligible* methods, so a method that is not enabled is
# simply not shown rather than raising an error. On a non-Thai account, use
# `card`.
#
#   STRIPE_PAYMENT_METHODS=card,promptpay   # default
#   STRIPE_PAYMENT_METHODS=card              # cards only, non-TH account
#   STRIPE_PAYMENT_METHODS=dynamic           # omit the filter; Stripe chooses
# STRIPE_PAYMENT_METHODS=

# The Stripe API version to pin, e.g. `2026-09-30.endive` — the version
# stripe-python 16.0.0 defaults to. Left unset here because a pin has to be a
# version the account accepts, which this server cannot check. Pinning is still
# the right thing for production: it stops an account-level default upgrade
# from changing a payload shape underneath working code.
# STRIPE_API_VERSION=

# How long to wait for Stripe, in seconds. Clamped to 2-30; default 10. Stripe's
# own SDK default is 80, which is far longer than someone on a paywall should
# ever wait.
# STRIPE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=

# --- Where a payment returns to ----------------------------------------------
# The origin Stripe sends the customer back to after paying. Leave it unset in
# development: the API already trusts loopback, and the app sends its own
# origin, so `npx expo start` on :8081 works out of the box.
#
# SET THIS IN PRODUCTION. A payment still succeeds and is still granted without
# it (the webhook does not depend on the redirect), but the customer lands on
# the fallback `http://localhost:8081` — a dead page right after paying. If
# CORS_ORIGINS is set, its first entry is used as the default, so a correctly
# configured deployment is usually already covered.
#
#   FRONTEND_BASE_URL=https://superheng-3yf.pages.dev
# FRONTEND_BASE_URL=

# --- CORS ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which browser origins may call this API. Leave these commented out for local
# development: the default is `*`, which is what the Expo web target on :8081
# and a physical phone on the LAN both need.
#
# In production set CORS_ORIGINS to the exact origin(s) of the hosted frontend
# — scheme, host and port, comma-separated, no trailing slash and no
# wildcards. An origin never includes a path, which is why
# `https://app.example.com` is correct and
# `https://app.example.com/dashboard` is not.
#
#   CORS_ORIGINS=https://superheng-3yf.pages.dev,https://app.superheng.app
#
# Cloudflare Pages gives every *preview* deployment a fresh
# `<hash>.<project>.pages.dev` host, so listing those individually is hopeless.
# Use CORS_ORIGIN_REGEX for them; it is matched against the origin in addition
# to the list above.
#
#   CORS_ORIGIN_REGEX=^https://([a-z0-9-]+\.)?superheng-3yf\.pages\.dev$
#
# Remember CORS is browser-only: it is not an access control, and curl, bots
# and the native app are unaffected by it.
# CORS_ORIGINS=
# CORS_ORIGIN_REGEX=

# Whether browsers are told they may send credentials (cookies, TLS client
# certs) with cross-origin requests. Anything other than 1/true/yes/on is false,
# and false is the default. The beta deployment sets this to true; see
# ../render.yaml.
#
# This is more subtle than the usual "never combine * with credentials" advice.
# Measured against Starlette 1.7.0 with `allow_origins=["*"]`:
#
#   CORS_ALLOW_CREDENTIALS unset -> access-control-allow-origin: *
#   CORS_ALLOW_CREDENTIALS=true  -> access-control-allow-origin: <caller origin>
#
# Starlette reflects the origin rather than emitting the invalid `*` plus
# credentials pair, so requests do not break. What it also means is that every
# origin on the internet is reflected *and* invited to send credentials — the
# exact protection a wildcard normally gives you is what turning this on
# removes.
#
# It is inert for this API today: authentication is a request header (the
# planned X-API-Key), not a cookie, so there is nothing for a browser to
# attach. It must be turned back off before any cookie-based session exists,
# alongside narrowing CORS_ORIGINS away from `*`.
# CORS_ALLOW_CREDENTIALS=
